; Scroll the title message, start the game, enter a room
; Three parts that share one entry.
; .
; Part 1 ($88AE-$88F9) continues the title screen of #R$87CA: it gets the result
; of the title tune (NZ if a start key stopped it) and, when the tune ended on
; its own, scrolls the message at #R$8454 along text line 19, one character per
; step, for $E0 steps (the message is 256 characters, the window 32; the last
; window equals the first, "+++++ Press ENTER to Start +++++"). Each step cycles
; the colours of all attribute cells with #R$8AEB (the border follows the first
; cell), prints the 32-character window in bright white on blue, plays a sound
; with #R$96DE and tests key 0 and ENTER (port $AFFE). A press ends the scroll
; and starts the game; when the scroll reaches the end the title screen is drawn
; again (JP #R$8813) and the tune restarts. Kempston fire is tested only while
; the tune plays (#R$96C9), not here. Observed: the whole scroll takes 843
; frames after the tune (frames 1582-2425 in the code.txt run).
; .
; Part 2 ($88FC) starts the game: resets the clock text at #R$857F from #R$8585
; and copies the status bar attributes from #R$9A00 to the bottom third of the
; screen, then falls into room entry.
; .
; Part 3 ($8912, entered at the start of the game and by every room change:
; #R$948A, #R$949E, #R$94B0, #R$94D2, the cheat teleport and the restart after a
; death in #R$8B07) loads room number #R$8420 from the room table: the 256 bytes
; at $C000 + 256 * room are copied to #R$8000. It then builds the entity buffer
; #R$8100 from the eight slots of the room (#R$80F0): for each slot, the 8-byte
; definition at $A000 + 8 * index (bit 7 of the index ignored) is copied, and
; the slot's second byte replaces byte 2 of the copy and of the definition. Then
; it saves Willy's state ($85CF-$85D5, 7 bytes) at $85D7 (the position to return
; to after a death), builds the empty-room buffers with #R$8D33, clears the
; bottom third of the bitmap, prints the room name (32 characters from #R$8080)
; on row 16 and the status line text #R$8554 on row 19, sets the border colour
; of the room, clears #R$85D6 and jumps to the game loop #R$89AD.
; .
; Hardware: reads ports $AFFE (keyboard) and, through the sound, writes port $FE
; (border, beeper); writes the screen bitmap and attributes.
@label=ScrollTitleMessage
c$88AE JP NZ,$88FC         ; NZ from #R$96A2: the tune was stopped by a start key
 $88B1 XOR A               ; scroll offset (#R$85E4) = 0
 $88B2 LD ($85E4),A        ;
@label=ScrollTitleMessage_Step
*$88B5 CALL $8AEB          ; cycle the colours of all attribute cells (#R$8AEB)
@isub=LD HL,TEXT_LINE_ATTR
 $88B8 LD HL,$5A60         ; row 19 attributes: bright white on blue
@isub=LD DE,TEXT_LINE_ATTR+1
 $88BB LD DE,$5A61         ;
@isub=LD BC,SCREEN_COLS-1
 $88BE LD BC,$001F         ;
@isub=LD (HL),BRIGHT_WHITE_ON_BLUE
 $88C1 LD (HL),$4F         ;
 $88C3 LDIR                ;
 $88C5 LD A,($85E4)        ;
 $88C8 LD IX,$8454         ; IX = message + scroll offset
 $88CC LD E,A              ;
 $88CD LD D,$00            ;
 $88CF ADD IX,DE           ;
 $88D1 LD DE,$5060         ;
@isub=LD C,SCREEN_COLS
 $88D4 LD C,$20            ;
 $88D6 CALL $9680          ; print the 32-character window on row 19
 $88D9 LD A,($85E4)        ;
 $88DC AND $1F             ; sound pitch depends on the position in the message
 $88DE ADD A,$32           ; A = (offset AND $1F) + $32
 $88E0 CALL $96DE          ; sweep sound #R$96DE
@isub=LD BC,KEYROW_START
 $88E3 LD BC,$AFFE         ;
 $88E6 IN A,(C)            ; key 0 or ENTER (bit 0 low when pressed)
 $88E8 AND $01             ;
 $88EA CP $01              ;
 $88EC JR NZ,$88FC         ; pressed: start the game
 $88EE LD A,($85E4)        ;
 $88F1 INC A               ; next step
@isub=CP TITLE_SCROLL_STEPS
 $88F2 CP $E0              ;
 $88F4 LD ($85E4),A        ;
 $88F7 JR NZ,$88B5         ; more steps
 $88F9 JP $8813            ; end of the message: draw the title screen again and replay the tune
